# exponentiation:
# CSES 1095, Exponentiation (https://cses.fi/problemset/task/1095), in our own
# words: base to the power exponent modulo 10^9 + 7. Part of the CSES port's first batch.
#
# THE PROGRAM, written by the definition: base^0 = 1 and base^(exponent+1) = base base^exponent, the exponent
# multiplications done on exact binary naturals and the product reduced
# modulo 10^9 + 7 at the end. Its reading takes exponent multiplications of a number
# of up to exponent log base bits; the replacement the family asks for is fast
# exponentiation, by the bits of exponent, on fixed-width integers reduced at each
# step.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ, application and
# top, with no choice and no parallel outside top. It β-reduces to V, the
# canonical binary numeral of the answer (the recursion consumes b): every top
# it holds (a bit of a literal that no key expects) stands in a branch that a
# Scott boolean discards, and binary_subtract, which writes no top
# (../common/binary_modular), is never asked for less than zero, since the
# program subtracts only what is no larger. So ⊢_β P = V, and P ≃_O V by the
# soundness of the derivable equations for the contextual preorder of Definition
# 14 (Lean `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V
# is an answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V
# ⊑_O W, while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W
# to λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
